# Join Validation Checklist

Before accepting a join, verify:

* [ ] Row count before the join is known
* [ ] Row count after the join is known
* [ ] Join keys are identified
* [ ] Primary and foreign key relationships are understood
* [ ] Expected one-to-one or one-to-many relationship is documented
* [ ] Unmatched keys are checked
* [ ] Unexpected row multiplication is investigated
* [ ] Aggregations are performed at the correct level
* [ ] Business purpose of the join is documented
* [ ] Data lineage is traceable

---

# Why Row Count Validation Matters

Row count validation prevents silent data-quality problems.

A join can produce more rows than expected because of:

* One-to-many relationships
* Duplicate keys
* Incorrect join conditions
* Many-to-many relationships
* Unexpected unmatched records

Without validation, these issues can lead to:

* Double-counted revenue
* Incorrect customer metrics
* Inflated transaction counts
* Incorrect business reports

Therefore, row counts should always be compared before and after important joins.

